# .swarm-policy.md.example
#
# Copy this to the ROOT of a project as `.swarm-policy.md` to give the
# llm-swarm-runner coordinator a per-project rules-of-engagement file.
# The coordinator reads it on every wake (Initial Startup Checklist step 1)
# and embeds it verbatim into every worker brief under a
# "## Project Guardrails (MUST OBEY)" header.
#
# Format: free-form markdown. The coordinator does not parse it; it just
# pastes the whole file into the worker brief. Be explicit and concrete.
# The worker (claude / gemini) reads it as part of its task and will refuse
# to violate clearly-stated rules in --yolo / --dangerously-skip-permissions
# mode (which otherwise has no other constraint).
#
# A baseline set of communication conventions (PR risk rating, end-of-work
# summary, decision-point framing, next-best-action hint, verbosity dial,
# "surface don't bury" notes, tiered self-merge by risk) is ALREADY injected
# before this file via `prompts/worker.md`. You don't need to repeat them;
# only override in the "Communication overrides" section below if your
# project needs something different.

---

## Pull-request rules
- PR titles must start with `[swarm]` so they're easy to filter in the UI.
- PR bodies must include the issue number being closed (`Closes #N`).
- Do not `gh pr review --approve` your own PR.
- Never `git push origin master` (or `main`) directly. Never `git push --force` to any protected branch.
- The default is **leave PRs OPEN for human review**. Self-merge is allowed only as described below.

### Merging your own PR

This project **defers to the tiered self-merge convention in `prompts/worker.md`** ("Merging your own PR" section). Summary so workers don't need to chase the link:

| PR risk | Default behavior |
|---|---|
| 🟢 **low** | Worker MAY propose a quick merge confirmation in the end-of-work summary (e.g. *"Merge PR #555 now? (yes / go / ship)"*). A short unhedged affirmative — `yes` / `y` / `go` / `do it` / `ship` / `merge` / `👍` — from the project lead authorizes self-merge. Hedged or off-topic replies do NOT count. |
| 🟡 **medium** | Worker MUST NOT propose merge. Run self-review (per `prompts/worker.md`) and surface the verdict in the handoff. **Only** self-merge on an unambiguous in-session instruction that **names the PR number** (e.g. *"merge PR 555"*, *"ship 555"*). A bare `yes` / `go` / `ship` is NOT enough — the user must have typed the PR number so a half-read affirmative can't trigger the merge. |
| 🔴 **high** | Worker NEVER self-merges, **even on explicit instruction**. Run self-review, then refuse and hand back the exact `gh pr merge` command for the project lead to run. There is no override keyword. |

**"Unambiguous instruction"** means a coordinator or project lead types **"merge PR N"** (or equivalent English that names the PR number) **in this session**. It is NOT inferable from:

- the PR's risk rating alone (low CAN auto-confirm via the affirmative-after-proposal path; medium and high CANNOT)
- prior session history or what other workers did on different PRs
- a bare `yes` / `go` / `ship` that wasn't preceded by your own (low-risk-only) merge proposal
- the worker's own self-assessment of "looks safe to me"
- the project lead implying you should merge ("looks good", "nice work")

If the project lead's instruction is ambiguous, ask in-pane. Don't infer.

**To disable self-merge entirely**, replace this section with a clause that explicitly says so (e.g. *"workers may not self-merge regardless of risk rating; always hand back the manual `gh pr merge` command"*). In that mode, even a low-risk merge proposal is forbidden — workers push the branch, open the PR, and hand back the command.

## File-modification rules
- Do **not** modify any of: `Dockerfile`, `docker-compose.yml`, `flyway/**`, `secrets/**`, `.env*`, `package-lock.json` (unless the task explicitly says so), `*.lock` (Gradle).
- Do **not** delete or rename files unless the task explicitly says so.
- Do **not** touch CI config (`.github/workflows/**`, `.gitlab-ci.yml`).

## Tool-use rules
- Do **not** run any `gradle` task matching `*release*`, `*publish*`, `*deploy*`.
- Do **not** run `kubectl`, `terraform`, `aws`, or any cloud CLI without explicit per-task approval.
- Database migrations are read-only research only — do not apply them.
- Network calls are fine for `gh`, `git`, `npm`, `pip`/`uv`, package registries; everything else is suspect.

## Concurrency rules
- Maximum 1 active worker per worktree.
- Do not spawn additional workers from inside a worker (no recursive provisioning).

## Communication rules
- If you (the worker) hit a real ambiguity that needs human input, **stop and ask in the pane**. Do not guess. Do not silently change scope.
- If the task is impossible as specified, write a short summary of why into `.agent-task-failed.md` and exit. Do not push a half-baked PR.
- Do not write any comments containing your model identifier or self-attribution unless explicitly asked.

## Communication overrides (optional)
This section overrides or extends the baseline conventions in
`prompts/worker.md` (which is injected above this file in every
worker brief). Use it for project-specific tuning. Examples of what could
go here:

- "Override default verbosity to `concise` for all workers in this repo."
- "PR risk rating must include a separate `<!-- LIQUIBASE_TOUCHED: yes|no -->` HTML comment when database migrations are present."
- "End-of-work summary must include a `## Coverage delta` section listing tests added/changed."
- "Next-best-action hints should always link to the project's runbook (`docs/RUNBOOK.md`) when CI fails."
- "**At-rest glyph enabled.** Emit `✅` as your final line when truly at rest, per `prompts/worker.md` § 'At-rest signal'."

Leave empty if the baseline is fine for this project.
